SUMMARY:The Invisible Scanners (Jim Grozier)
LOCATION:Poynting Large Lecture Theatre\, Physics Building (R13)
DESCRIPTION:Jim Grozier\nInstitute of Physics Public Lecture Series\n\nThis
  talk covers a period in the recent history of particle physics (approxima
 tely the 1960s and 1970s) when the output of particle colliders was in the
  form of thousands of photographs of bubble chamber events\, and large num
 bers of relatively unskilled women were brought in to examine and analyse 
 these photographs in search of patterns which might reveal evidence of new
  particles or unusual decays. These women were largely “invisible” in 
 that they were not named in research papers and received little reward\, d
 espite the fact that they were at the forefront of discovery. In this\, th
 eir story bears some comparison with those of the “Harvard Computers” 
 – women who analysed astronomical…\n\nDetails: https://www.birmingham.
 ac.uk/about/college-of-engineering-and-physical-sciences/physics-and-astro
 nomy/outreach-and-schools-liaison/outreach-for-secondary-schools/iop-publi
 c-lecture-series
CATEGORIES:Institute of Physics Public Lecture Series
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://www.birmingham.ac.uk/about/college-of-engineering-and-physical-
 sciences/physics-and-astronomy/outreach-and-schools-liaison/outreach-for-s
 econdary-schools/iop-public-lecture-series
END:VEVENT
